-Runtime: Linux backend only. No UI or Apple platform. Retry attempts run without a limit and retry authentication failures. Desired policy: at most 3 attempts total, retry transient failures only, preserve job ID. Current policy: {"maxAttempts":null,"retryStatus":[401,403,408,429,500,502,503],"preserveJobId":false}.+# Queue retry policy
+
+Runtime: Linux backend service. This policy is framework-neutral.
+
+```json
+{
+  "maxAttempts": 3,
+  "retryStatus": [408, 429, 500, 502, 503],
+  "preserveJobId": true
+}
+```
+
+- `maxAttempts` counts the initial execution and all retries together: at most three executions (one initial attempt and up to two retries).
+- Retry only failures with a status in `retryStatus`, treated as the transient-failure allowlist. Authentication/authorization failures (401 and 403), unlisted statuses, and failures without a classified status do not trigger a retry.
+- Stop immediately on success or a nonretryable failure. After the third failed execution, stop even if the status is retryable and record the job as failed/exhausted.
+- Preserve the original job ID on every attempt. Retries must not create a new logical job or reset its attempt count. Persist the ID and consumed attempt count across worker restarts or redelivery so these cannot bypass the limit.
+
+The supplied requirements do not define retry timing, backoff, or handling of `Retry-After`; those remain unspecified. Job ID preservation alone does not guarantee idempotent side effects.
